Multi-Cloud Strategies with Terraform
Objectives
Design and deploy a modular Terraform configuration that provisions compute and networking resources simultaneously across AWS, Azure, and GCP
Implement provider-specific Terraform modules with consistent tagging, output contracts, and variable interfaces
Validate cross-cloud infrastructure state using CLI tooling and Terraform outputs
Prerequisites
Comfort with Linux command-line operations including file editing, directory navigation, and shell scripting
Conceptual understanding of cloud networking primitives: virtual networks, subnets, security groups, and public IP assignment
Active accounts on AWS, Azure, and GCP with permissions to create compute instances, virtual networks, and firewall rules
Lab Environment
You will work on a dedicated AWS EC2 Ubuntu instance provided by Al Nafi. The instance has a base Ubuntu installation; you will install all required tools in Task 1.

Task 1: Install and Verify the Multi-Cloud Toolchain
Requirement 1
Install Terraform, the AWS CLI, the Azure CLI, and the Google Cloud CLI on the bare Ubuntu instance, then configure credentials for all three providers using environment variables or CLI authentication flows so that each provider can reach its respective control plane.

After completing this step, the following four commands must each return a version string with exit code 0:

terraform version
aws --version
az version
gcloud version
Official installation references (use these if any URL below has changed):

Terraform: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/install
AWS CLI v2: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/getting-started-install.html
Azure CLI: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/install-azure-cli-linux
Google Cloud CLI: https://cloud.google.com/sdk/docs/install
# 1. System baseline
sudo apt-get update -y && sudo apt-get install -y curl wget unzip gnupg lsb-release jq
# 2. Terraform
TERRAFORM_VERSION="1.8.5"
wget -fsSL "https://releases.hashicorp.com/terraform/${TERRAFORM_VERSION}/terraform_${TERRAFORM_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip" \
  -O /tmp/terraform.zip
unzip /tmp/terraform.zip -d /tmp/terraform_bin
sudo mv /tmp/terraform_bin/terraform /usr/local/bin/terraform
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/terraform
terraform version
Troubleshoot this step:

You may see: Error: Failed to query available provider packages or terraform: command not found if the binary was not moved correctly.
Confirm the binary is on PATH: echo $PATH must include /usr/local/bin; run ls -lh /usr/local/bin/terraform to confirm the file exists and is executable.
Official guide: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/install
# 3. AWS CLI v2
curl -fsSL "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o /tmp/awscliv2.zip
unzip /tmp/awscliv2.zip -d /tmp/awscli
sudo /tmp/awscli/aws/install
aws --version
Troubleshoot this step:

You may see: aws: command not found or install failed if a previous version of the AWS CLI is present at /usr/bin/aws.
Run which aws to locate any conflicting binary; if /usr/bin/aws exists, pass --update flag: sudo /tmp/awscli/aws/install --update.
Official guide: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/getting-started-install.html
# 4. Azure CLI
curl -fsSL https://aka.ms/InstallAzureCLIDeb | sudo bash
