Task 2: Design and Deploy the Multi-Cloud Terraform Module Architecture
Problem Statement
Design and implement a Terraform project that provisions one compute instance with a public IP address, one virtual network with a single public subnet, and one firewall/security-group permitting inbound TCP on ports 22 and 80 â simultaneously across AWS, Azure, and GCP â using a separate child module for each provider. The root module must call all three child modules, and every resource across all three clouds must carry a consistent set of tags or labels containing at minimum the keys environment and project.

Phase 1: Module Architecture and Variable Contract
Design a directory layout with a root module and three child modules (modules/aws, modules/azure, modules/gcp). Each child module must declare its own variables.tf and outputs.tf. The root module must pass a shared environment and project_name variable down to all three child modules, and each child module must output at minimum the public IP address of its compute instance.

Acceptance Criteria

Running terraform validate from the root module directory exits with code 0 and prints Success! The configuration is valid. with no warnings; this must pass before terraform plan is attempted.
Running terraform fmt -check -recursive from the root module directory exits with code 0, confirming all .tf files are correctly formatted; any file that causes a non-zero exit must be reformatted with terraform fmt -recursive until the check passes.
Phase 2: Provision and Verify All Three Clouds
Deploy the infrastructure with terraform apply and verify that all three compute instances are reachable on port 80 using curl.

Acceptance Criteria

After terraform apply completes, running terraform output -json must produce a JSON object containing a non-empty public IP address for each of the three cloud providers; a null or empty string value for any provider's IP is a failure.
Each of the three public IP addresses returned by terraform output must respond to curl -fsSL --max-time 10 http://<IP>/ with HTTP 200 and an HTML body containing the word Hello; a connection timeout or non-200 response means the web server user-data script did not execute correctly and must be debugged before the phase is considered complete.
Verification commands to run after apply:

# Extract IPs from Terraform output and test each one
AWS_IP=$(terraform output -json | jq -r '.aws_instance_public_ip.value')
AZURE_IP=$(terraform output -json | jq -r '.azure_public_ip.value')
GCP_IP=$(terraform output -json | jq -r '.gcp_instance_external_ip.value')

echo "AWS IP:   $AWS_IP"
echo "Azure IP: $AZURE_IP"
echo "GCP IP:   $GCP_IP"

curl -fsSL --max-time 10 "http://${AWS_IP}/"
curl -fsSL --max-time 10 "http://${AZURE_IP}/"
curl -fsSL --max-time 10 "http://${GCP_IP}/"
Phase 3: State Inspection and Cross-Cloud Consistency Audit
Without modifying any .tf files, use terraform state commands and each provider's CLI to confirm that the resources Terraform believes it created actually exist in each cloud's control plane, and that the environment and project tags are present on every compute instance.

Acceptance Criteria

Running terraform state list must return at least six resource addresses â a minimum of two per provider (one network resource and one compute resource each) â and terraform state show <address> on each compute instance resource must display the public IP that matches the corresponding terraform output value.
Each cloud CLI command below must return the tag/label values matching what was set in terraform.tfvars; a missing tag on any resource is a failure requiring a terraform apply fix before the lab is considered complete.
# AWS: verify tags on the EC2 instance
aws ec2 describe-instances \
  --filters "Name=tag:Project,Values=*" \
  --query 'Reservations[*].Instances[*].{ID:InstanceId,State:State.Name,Tags:Tags}' \
  --output json | jq '.[][] | {id: .ID, state: .State, tags: .Tags}'

# Azure: verify tags on the VM
az vm list --show-details \
  --query '[*].{name:name, tags:tags, publicIps:publicIps}' \
  --output json | jq '.[] | {name: .name, tags: .tags}'

# GCP: verify labels on the compute instance
gcloud compute instances list \
  --format="json" | jq '.[] | {name: .name, labels: .labels, ip: .networkInterfaces[0].accessConfigs[0].natIP}'
Expected Outcomes
A working Terraform project with three child modules that can be initialized, planned, applied, and destroyed cleanly, with terraform destroy removing all resources across all three clouds without manual intervention.
Demonstrated ability to cross-reference Terraform state with live cloud-provider CLI output, confirming that IaC-managed resources match their declared configuration.
Conclusion
This lab required you to design a non-trivial multi-provider Terraform architecture from first principles, manage authentication across three distinct cloud control planes, and validate infrastructure state using both Terraform's own state commands and native CLI tooling. The discipline of separating provider logic into child modules with explicit variable and output contracts is the foundation of maintainable multi-cloud IaC at production scale. Before leaving the lab environment, run terraform destroy to remove all provisioned resources and avoid unexpected cloud charges.
